WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.600
The double helix revealed the shape of DNA.

2
00:00:03.960 --> 00:00:07.620
But how can scientists read base order when shape does not show it?

3
00:00:08.820 --> 00:00:10.400
Move the yellow lens to order.

4
00:00:13.833 --> 00:00:15.573
Start with one template strand.

5
00:00:16.813 --> 00:00:20.633
A copying enzyme moves along it, adding matching base blocks.

6
00:00:22.053 --> 00:00:24.093
Ordinary blocks let the copy keep growing.

7
00:00:25.313 --> 00:00:27.273
The letters are still hidden in the process.

8
00:00:28.417 --> 00:00:32.977
Sanger's original method ran a separate reaction for each kind of base.

9
00:00:34.377 --> 00:00:39.317
Each reaction mixed ordinary blocks with a small amount of one kind of stop block.

10
00:00:40.597 --> 00:00:44.077
When a stop block joins a copy, that copy cannot grow farther.

11
00:00:45.417 --> 00:00:49.857
Many copies grow at once, so copies stop at many different matching positions.

12
00:00:51.417 --> 00:00:55.777
Later versions give each kind of stop block its own color and use one reaction.

13
00:00:56.850 --> 00:01:00.250
Each tray now holds a mixture, not one planned staircase.

14
00:01:01.650 --> 00:01:03.930
Each copy ends with the base that stopped it.

15
00:01:05.150 --> 00:01:08.430
Sorting lanes can turn those lengths into readable order.

16
00:01:09.483 --> 00:01:11.343
Predict before the lane runs.

17
00:01:12.543 --> 00:01:16.903
Which copy reaches the bottom first: a shorter copy or a longer copy?

18
00:01:18.163 --> 00:01:19.363
Hold the picture and choose.

19
00:01:25.417 --> 00:01:29.557
All the copies start moving together, but shorter copies move faster.

20
00:01:30.857 --> 00:01:34.457
The shortest reaches the bottom first, then longer copies follow.

21
00:01:35.857 --> 00:01:40.877
Reading each stop base in that order reveals the complementary sequence, one

22
00:01:40.877 --> 00:01:42.077
position after another.

23
00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:45.660
Sequencing changes the useful model.

24
00:01:46.920 --> 00:01:48.860
A coiled shape becomes a letter string.

25
00:01:50.140 --> 00:01:55.060
That string shows identity and order clearly, while hiding shape, size, and the

26
00:01:55.060 --> 00:01:56.060
partner strand.

27
00:01:56.933 --> 00:02:00.333
A genome is all the DNA in one chromosome set.

28
00:02:01.633 --> 00:02:03.693
A reference sequence acts like a ruler.

29
00:02:05.013 --> 00:02:09.273
New sequence strips align against it, making differences easier to find.

30
00:02:10.367 --> 00:02:16.067
The Human Genome Project used improved Sanger sequencing to build a human reference.

31
00:02:17.447 --> 00:02:20.687
It joined work from several donors and still left gaps.

32
00:02:22.247 --> 00:02:24.907
Later projects filled more gaps and added variation.

33
00:02:26.067 --> 00:02:28.487
Reading also became far less costly.

34
00:02:29.967 --> 00:02:33.727
Plot the lesson's production-cost estimates on a logarithmic scale.

35
00:02:35.007 --> 00:02:37.387
The computed curve plunges, then flattens.

36
00:02:38.350 --> 00:02:39.830
The curve needs limits.

37
00:02:41.130 --> 00:02:44.530
These are production estimates, not prices paid by a person.

38
00:02:46.170 --> 00:02:48.470
Later estimates use an existing reference.

39
00:02:50.150 --> 00:02:52.130
Earlier estimates priced building without one.

40
00:02:53.430 --> 00:02:55.210
Interpretation costs are excluded.

41
00:02:56.350 --> 00:02:59.750
Lower cost means a fixed budget can read more genomes.

42
00:03:01.250 --> 00:03:06.050
Biologists can compare many people and species instead of relying on one reference.

43
00:03:07.350 --> 00:03:09.550
The reference remains a model with limits.

44
00:03:10.650 --> 00:03:12.590
Shape required a structure model.

45
00:03:14.130 --> 00:03:15.670
Order required a sequence model.

46
00:03:17.450 --> 00:03:18.750
Comparison required a reference.

47
00:03:20.190 --> 00:03:24.130
Choose DNA models by the question; every model leaves features out.
